{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788382412,"nonce":"E3v0rA5B/zTUQDaTP1IX7RvJfes=","title":"Brainstorm, opened from play (run openrun-20260902, day 6). Not a bug report —","message":"four separate verbs each behave correctly on their own and the COMPOSITION of\nthem is what a colony actually needs and cannot express.\n\n\nA new colonist arrived (Fitz, Shooting 8) who should hold the bolt-action\nrifle currently carried by Aaron (Shooting 3, Trigger-happy). Ellis (Shooting 0)\nholds the revolver that should go to Aaron. That is one sentence of intent:\n\n    rifle -\u003e Fitz ; revolver -\u003e Aaron ; knife -\u003e Ellis\n\nExpressing it took six calls, produced one stranded weapon 250 cells from base,\nand could not be completed at all while the game was paused.\n\n\n1. **`equip` / `wear` / `drop` are DIRECT orders that replace the running job.**\n   Five orders were sent at colony start; three were silently discarded because\n   each replaced the last. The envelope says `order_effect: \"started\"`,\n   `queue: false`, `queue_depth: 0` — honest, but only if you read it. `queue:true`\n   exists and fixes it. **The default is the lossy one.**\n\n2. **An item held by a pawn is not addressable.** `equip {thing:4037}` on a\n   revolver another colonist is carrying returns\n   `\"no visible thing with id 4037 on the current map (things in unexplored\n   ground are not reported)\"`. So a transfer is necessarily drop-then-equip,\n   which means...\n\n3. **...it needs the clock to run.** `drop` queues a `DropEquipment` job. While\n   paused the item is still equipped, still not addressable, and the follow-up\n   `equip` refuses. So **a multi-pawn reallocation cannot be composed while the\n   game is paused** — which is the only state the agent ever plans in. The\n   loop's own contract is \"the agent owns time\"; this is a class of act that\n   requires spending time mid-plan.\n\n4. **A queued order cannot be cancelled without drafting.** Having queued a\n   `drop` for a pawn 250 cells from base, the natural correction is to give him\n   a different order. `move-to` refuses: `\"this order is only offered to a\n   drafted pawn\"`. Correct reproduction of the widget gate, and it means the\n   only undo for a misaimed equipment order is to draft, order, undraft.\n\nNet effect: the revolver was dropped at (3,231), the far map corner, and now\nneeds a hauling round trip that no one asked for.\n\n\n**An allocation verb that takes an end state, not a job.** Something shaped\nlike:\n\n    equip-plan {\n      assign: [ {pawn: 12659, thing: 4036},      # or {pawn:.., def:\"Gun_...\"}\n                {pawn: 1018,  thing: 4037},\n                {pawn: 1022,  def:\"MeleeWeapon_Knife\"} ],\n      dry_run: true }\n\nand the mod computes the job chain — who must drop what, who walks where, in\nwhat order — and either enqueues the whole thing atomically or refuses with the\nreason per row. Properties worth having:\n\n- **Addresses items wherever they are**: on the ground, in a stockpile, in a\n  pawn's inventory, equipped by another pawn. The \"not visible\" refusal above is\n  the single sharpest edge.\n- **Resolves by def as well as by id**, because the caller usually means \"the\n  best rifle we own\", not \"thing 4036\".\n- **Plans while paused.** Enqueue the chain; let the next `advance` execute it.\n  This is the property that matters most — planning happens at zero ticks.\n- **`dry_run` that reports the chain** before committing, the way\n  `place-layout --dry-run` already does for construction. Equipment deserves the\n  same courtesy: it is a transaction over several pawns.\n- **Idempotent.** Re-sending the same allocation when it is already satisfied\n  should be a no-op that says so, not another five jobs.\n\nSame argument extends to apparel (`wear`) and to hauling a specific thing to a\nspecific place, which is why \"pickup / equip / drop engine\" is the right scope\nrather than a weapons-only verb.\n\n\nArmament is re-derived at every roster change, every raid and every death\n(`triggered.md` §roster-change, §raid-end, `daily.md` §armed-roster), and G3 of\nthe current run contract grades on it directly. It is one of the most frequent\nmulti-pawn transactions a colony performs and it is currently the least\nexpressible. `place-layout` exists because \"preflight every cell first; on any\nfailure place NOTHING\" cannot hold across N `build` calls — the identical\nargument applies here across N `equip` calls.\n\n\n- A single call reallocates weapons across the whole roster from a stated end\n  state, **issued while the game is paused**, and the next `advance` carries it\n  out.\n- Items equipped by another pawn, in inventory, and in a stockpile are all\n  addressable.\n- `dry_run` prints the resolved job chain and every refusal, with the same\n  `{gate, reason}` shape the other verbs use.\n- Re-issuing a satisfied allocation reports `already-satisfied` and enqueues\n  nothing.\n- The existing verbs keep working; this composes them rather than replacing\n  them.","files":null}]}